  "response": "**Setup**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(–|¬D) = 0.95, so false positive rate P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**Bayes' theorem**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)P(D) + P(+|¬D)P(¬D)\n= (0.98)(0.002) + (0.05)(0.998)\n= 0.00196 + 0.0499\n= 0.05186\n\nP(D|+) = P(+|D)P(D) / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\nConverting to a percentage: **3.78%**\n\nThis illustrates the classic result that even with a fairly sensitive/specific test, low base-rate conditions yield a low positive predictive value — most positives are false positives here.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
